\startsection[title={\titlecite{uzbas2019}}]

% flexible processing pipeline and Python package
Summary:
In this article, we developed BART-Seq, a targeted RNA-Seq protocol usable at the single cell or bulk levels.
See \inlinecite[uzbas2019] and \insec[sec:bart-seq] for BART-Seq, a transcriptomics protocol that allows to quantify many samples at once.
It is set apart from untargeted single cell and bulk RNA-Seq protocols due to its high sensitivity and low cost.
BART-Seq can use single cells or groups of multiple cells as samples.
Each of its samples is tagged by combining paired-end reads with pairs of computationally designed barcodes.
It is therefore possible distinguish high numbers of samples while sensitively measuring the specified target amplicons, such as expressed genes.
An advantage of the predictable barcoding over randomized barcodes is that sample identity is maintained, allowing for arbitrarily much information attached to each sample of a single batch, such as treatment labels.
BART-Seq has an unique demultiplexing and counting strategy that can be tuned for sensitivity or conservativity (see \insec[sec:bart-seq]).
The counting strategies and visualization routines for the protocol are available as a Python package allowing fine grained programmatic access \cite[angerer2019a].
Part of this package is the BART-Seq equivalent to \n{Cell Ranger} or \n{zUMIs}:
a \n{Snakemake} pipeline \cite[koster2012] that performs all steps of the protocol to create count matrices, statistics and visualizations.

My contribution:
I designed the demultiplexing and counting strategy together with Fatma Uzbas and Micha Drukker.
I implemented the Python package, and built the pipeline wrapping the package for use with minimal configuration.
For the publication, I wrote the section about demultiplexing and contributed to the remaining sections, mainly to the primer design section.
I demultiplexed the final version of all data used in the publication using my pipeline.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2020}}]

Summary:
% combines gene-level with cell-level analysis
In this article, we present gene relevance, a novel method for inference of the contributions individual variables have on the location of data in an embedding.
This algorithm is the latest addition to the \n{destiny} package, building upon its efficient routines.
The method was inspired by PCA loadings, a intrinsic property of the linear dimensionality reduction method PCA.
These loadings are the linear factors scaling a variable to define its role in a principal component.
Using PCA as dimensional reduction method for expression data therefore allows to see each gene’s contribution to a cell’s position in a plot.
Gene relevance generalizes the idea to make it applicable to nonlinear dimension reduction methods.
It is designed to identify genes that drive the local positioning of a cell in an embedding by calculating the differential of each gene-cell combination.
This involves estimating the partial derivatives of the gene expression with respect to the embedding location by looking at cell neighborhoods.
This allows to detect locally relevant genes without a discrete clustering step, and therefore without data reuse as a result of using inferred cluster labels as input for gene level analysis.
The article shows robust derivation of both obvious candidates such as hemoglobin genes for hematopoietic development, as well as interesting but less obvious genes.
We also present an easy to use interface for calculating gene lists and plotting as part of the \n{destiny} package.

My contribution:
The initial idea comes from Fabian Theis, while I designed the changes making it applicable to real data with A. Scialdone and C. Marr.
I implemented the algorithm as an extension of \n{destiny}’s data structures and API,
offering a rich and user friendly way to interact with for all gene relevance concepts, including plotting routines.
I also extended the idea to include scores for defined groups of cells (such as clusters) or the whole data for a global relevance score per gene.
The method is described in detail in \insec[sec:gene-relevance].
For the publication, I designed and performed its data analysis, which Antonio Scialdone interpreted.
I wrote the first draft, while David Fischer and Carsten Marr contributed to the mathematical description.
I completed writing the manuscript with feedback from Carsten Marr.

\stopsection

